/OPBaseFont2 13 Tf
(TO THE HONORABLE SPECIAL COURT OF REVIEW:) Tj
1 0 0 1 108 678.2 Tm
105 Tz
/OPBaseFont0 13 Tf
(The Honorable Sharon Keller, Presiding Judge of the Texas Court of Criminal) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 648.25 Tm
101 Tz
(Appeals \(the “CCA”\), files this Motion seeking dismissal of all charges asserted against) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 618.25 Tm
103 Tz
(her in the Charging Document filed by the State Commission on Judicial Conduct \(the) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.5 588.45 Tm
102 Tz
(“Commission” or “SCJC”\), as well as dismissal of the SCJC’s July 16, 2010, Order and) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 558.7 Tm
99 Tz
(public warning \(the “Order,” included at Appendix A\).) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.25 528.45 Tm
121 Tz
/OPBaseFont2 13 Tf
(I. INTRODUCTION) Tj
1 0 0 1 108 501.599 Tm
101 Tz
/OPBaseFont0 13 Tf
(Even when conducted flawlessly, judicial disciplinary proceedings skirt the edges) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.25 471.849 Tm
98 Tz
(of due process:) Tj
1 0 0 1 151.9 477.849 Tm
52 Tz
(1) Tj
1 0 0 1 154.3 471.599 Tm
101 Tz
( the SCJC acts as police, grand jury, prosecutor, judge, and jury, because) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 441.849 Tm
104 Tz
(it investigates alleged misconduct, brings charges, prosecutes cases, rules on motions,) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.5 411.849 Tm
111 Tz
(and determines punishments; allegations against judges are made anonymously;) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.25 382.049 Tm
102 Tz
(discovery is limited; judges may be punished without a hearing; judges have no right to) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.7 302.399 Tm
73 Tz
(1 ) Tj
1 0 0 1 79.9 302.199 Tm
100 Tz
(Article 5, Section 1-a\(11\) of the Texas Constitution provides that judges in disciplinary) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 287.25 Tm
103 Tz
(proceedings must be accorded due process “in the same manner that any person whose) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 272.199 Tm
100 Tz
(property rights are in jeopardy in an adjudicatory proceeding is entitled to due process of) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.25 257.25 Tm
(law[.]” As one court observed:) Tj
1 0 0 1 108.95 236.399 Tm
113 Tz
([J]udges are afforded the full benefit of the procedural due process) Tj
1 0 0 1 108 221.549 Tm
103 Tz
(protections of the Fourteenth Amendment, which, ‘though a variable and) Tj
1 0 0 1 108.25 206.399 Tm
109 Tz
(elusive concept, in its basic essence means a fair process.’ Cohn, The) Tj
1 0 0 1 108 191.549 Tm
107 Tz
(Limited Due Process Rights of Judges In Disciplinary Proceedings, 63) Tj
1 0 0 1 108 176.649 Tm
115 Tz
(Judicature 232, 235 \(1979\). These protections include the right to) Tj
1 0 0 1 108.25 161.549 Tm
101 Tz
(discovery and the privilege against self-incrimination, Cohn, ) Tj
1 0 0 1 432 161.5 Tm
129 Tz
/OPBaseFont3 11 Tf
(Id. ) Tj
1 0 0 1 450.95 161.549 Tm
98 Tz
/OPBaseFont0 13 Tf
(at 238-40,) Tj
1 0 0 1 108.5 146.649 Tm
116 Tz
(and are derived from the liberty and property interests judges are) Tj
1 0 0 1 108 131.799 Tm
105 Tz
(recognized to have in their offices. Shamen, et al., Judicial Conduct and) Tj
1 0 0 1 108 116.899 Tm
104 Tz
(Ethics, § 13.09 at 448. Fundamental fairness, therefore, is a concept that) Tj
1 0 0 1 108 101.799 Tm
99 Tz
(pervades the judicial disciplinary process.) Tj
1 0 0 1 71.75 80.849 Tm
118 Tz
/OPBaseFont3 11 Tf
(In re Cicchetti, ) Tj
1 0 0 1 153.6 80.849 Tm
99 Tz
/OPBaseFont0 13 Tf
(697 A.2d 297, 307 \(Pa. Ct. Jud. Disc.1997\).) Tj
